// PROBE: does STRICT run the decision gate, or only `--fast`?
//
// `k < succ(k + t)` is TRUE over ℕ and FALSE over ℤ (at t = -1 it is k < k). The
// `arithmetic` decision is sort-agnostic and reads variables as integers unless a `nonneg`
// predicate resolves in scope — this file deliberately has none — so the decision REFUTES it.
//
// The certifier, meanwhile, CAN build a valid ℕ proof of it: it cites `addSuccRight` and the
// order-introduction lemma, whose ℕ-ness is baked into the lemmas rather than assumed of the
// variables. So before the fix the two modes DISAGREED — strict emitted a kernel-checked
// certificate and passed, `--fast` ran the decision and failed.
//
// Strict must apply every gate `--fast` applies and THEN additionally certify, so both modes
// must now reject this with the same diagnostic. A regression here means the decision has
// been moved back inside `if (self.admit_mode)`.
import peano <<< "std/peano.b4m"

sort Nat = peano.Nat
func succ = peano.succ
func add = peano.add
pred less_than = peano.less_than

theorem indexFact: forall k, t: Nat; less_than(k, succ(add(k, t)))
proof
  @conclusion |
    forall k, t: Nat; less_than(k, succ(add(k, t)))
    [using arithmetic(peano)]
qed
